{#
  The application-consistent backup for one Mixpost instance: a CronJob in the instance's
  own namespace that dumps its database, archives its uploads, and pushes both into the
  lab's existing PBS datastore with proxmox-backup-client.

  Rendered by roles/mixpost/tasks/main.yml and applied through
  tasks/kubernetes/apply-manifest.yml. Owned by the namespace like everything else the
  instance owns, so removal deletes it and nothing has to remember it exists.

  WHY THIS EXISTS WHEN PBS ALREADY SNAPSHOTS THE CLUSTER NODES

  PBS backs up the k3s VMs on the lab's normal nightly schedule. That is the node rebuild
  path and nothing more: a whole-VM image is crash-consistent, it is taken with no
  knowledge of what MySQL was doing at the time, and it cannot address a single
  application's volume for a restore. Recovering one app's database from it means
  restoring an entire cluster node beside the live one and digging the files out. The
  artifact that actually restores an application is produced here, by the application's
  own database, in the application's own namespace.

  NO CREDENTIAL APPEARS IN THIS FILE, AND NONE APPEARS IN ANY COMMAND LINE

  The PBS token secret and the MySQL root password live in the Secret that
  tasks/kubernetes/sync-secret.yml wrote from the canonical Vaultwarden items. The PBS
  secret reaches the client as PBS_PASSWORD, which is the documented environment input the
  client reads instead of prompting. The MySQL password never becomes an argument or an
  environment variable at all: it is mounted as a `[client]` stanza and handed to mysqldump
  with --defaults-extra-file, so it is not visible in `ps`, in the Job's argv, or in
  anything the job prints. Both are deleted with the namespace.
#}
{% set b = app_config.backup %}
{#
  One set of labels, emitted at three different depths. Held UNINDENTED and placed with
  `indent`, because a block carrying its own leading spaces is only correct at the one
  depth it was written for: pasted verbatim under `jobTemplate.metadata` it produced
  four-space children of a six-space key, and the whole manifest was rejected by the YAML
  parser before Kubernetes ever saw it.
#}
{% set labels %}
app.kubernetes.io/instance: {{ instance }}
app.kubernetes.io/managed-by: homelab-infra
app.kubernetes.io/component: backup
{% endset %}
---
apiVersion: batch/v1
kind: CronJob
metadata:
  name: {{ instance }}-backup
  labels:
{{ labels | trim | indent(4, true) }}
spec:
  schedule: "{{ b.schedule }}"
  # Without this the schedule is read in the controller manager's timezone, which is UTC
  # here, and the operator who wrote "03:00" gets a job at some other hour of their night.
  timeZone: "{{ _mp_backup_timezone }}"
  # Forbid, not Allow. Two concurrent runs would dump the same database twice into the same
  # PBS group and, worse, both mount the storage volume — and the second one would be
  # starting precisely because the first is stuck, which is when piling on helps least.
  concurrencyPolicy: Forbid
  # A cluster that was down over the scheduled time still runs the backup when it comes
  # back, provided it comes back within the hour. Without a deadline a missed schedule is
  # skipped outright and the first night after an outage silently has no backup.
  startingDeadlineSeconds: 3600
  successfulJobsHistoryLimit: 3
  # Kept deliberately: a failed backup's pod is the only place its reason exists, and the
  # operator normally learns about it from the notification hours later.
  failedJobsHistoryLimit: 3
  jobTemplate:
    metadata:
      labels:
{{ labels | trim | indent(8, true) }}
    spec:
      # Two retries, then stop and stay failed. A backup that cannot reach PBS will not
      # start reaching it by being retried thirty times, and the default backoffLimit of 6
      # turns one bad night into six identical failure notifications.
      backoffLimit: 2
      activeDeadlineSeconds: {{ b.timeout_seconds | int }}
      template:
        metadata:
          labels:
{{ labels | trim | indent(12, true) }}
        spec:
          # Never, not OnFailure. OnFailure restarts the containers inside the same pod and
          # each restart overwrites the log of the attempt before it; a fresh pod per
          # attempt keeps every attempt's output readable.
          restartPolicy: Never

          # ── Step 1: the quiesced dump ────────────────────────────────────────
          # An initContainer rather than a second container, because the ordering is the
          # whole point: containers in a pod start together, initContainers run to
          # completion first. The upload must not begin until the dump has finished.
          #
          # WHY mysqldump --single-transaction IS THE RIGHT QUIESCE HERE
          #
          # Mixpost is a Laravel application and every table it creates is InnoDB.
          # --single-transaction opens one REPEATABLE READ transaction and takes the whole
          # dump inside it, so every table is read at the same point in the database's
          # history, without taking a global read lock and without the application seeing
          # a single moment of downtime. The alternatives are worse for this workload:
          # --lock-all-tables blocks writes for the length of the dump, and a file-level
          # copy of /var/lib/mysql taken while mysqld is running is not a backup at all,
          # it is the crash-consistent image this whole CronJob exists to replace.
          #
          # It runs from the SAME image the database itself runs, not from a distribution
          # package. A MariaDB client — which is what `default-mysql-client` is on Debian —
          # cannot authenticate to MySQL 8 with its default caching_sha2_password plugin,
          # and the failure reads as a wrong password rather than a wrong client. Using the
          # server's own image makes client and server the same build by construction, and
          # the node has already pulled it to run the database.
          initContainers:
            - name: dump
              image: {{ app_config.app.database.image }}
              command:
                - /bin/sh
                - -c
                - |
                  set -eu
                  umask 077
                  # --defaults-extra-file MUST be the first argument; mysqldump reads it
                  # before it parses anything else and ignores it anywhere later.
                  mysqldump \
                    --defaults-extra-file=/etc/backup-credentials/my.cnf \
                    --host="${DB_HOST}" \
                    --single-transaction \
                    --routines --triggers --events \
                    --hex-blob \
                    --set-gtid-purged=OFF \
                    --databases "${DB_NAME}" \
                    > /artifact/database.sql
                  echo "dumped ${DB_NAME}: $(wc -c < /artifact/database.sql) bytes"
              env:
                - name: DB_HOST
                  value: {{ instance }}-db
                - name: DB_NAME
                  value: {{ app_config.app.database.name }}
              volumeMounts:
                - name: credentials
                  mountPath: /etc/backup-credentials
                  readOnly: true
                - name: artifact
                  mountPath: /artifact
              resources:
                requests:
                  cpu: 100m
                  memory: 256Mi

          # ── Step 2: push both artifacts into PBS ─────────────────────────────
          containers:
            - name: push
              image: {{ b.client.image }}
              # IfNotPresent, so the first successful run caches the image on the node and
              # a registry outage at 03:00 does not by itself cost a backup.
              imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent
              command:
                - /bin/sh
                - -c
                - |
                  set -eu
                  export DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive

                  # Never `set -x` in this script. The client reads its credential from
                  # PBS_PASSWORD, and a trace would print the environment into the job log.

                  notify() {
                    [ -n "${NTFY_URL:-}" ] || return 0
                    if [ -n "${NTFY_TOKEN:-}" ]; then
                      curl -fsS -m 15 -X POST \
                        -H "Title: $1" -H "Priority: high" \
                        -H "Authorization: Bearer ${NTFY_TOKEN}" \
                        -d "$2" "${NTFY_URL}" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
                    else
                      curl -fsS -m 15 -X POST \
                        -H "Title: $1" -H "Priority: high" \
                        -d "$2" "${NTFY_URL}" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
                    fi
                  }
                  # The whole reason a nightly job notifies at all: a backup that stops
                  # working stops quietly. The operator finds out at restore time unless
                  # something says so on the night it happened.
                  on_exit() {
                    if [ "$1" -eq 0 ]; then
                      return 0
                    fi
                    notify "Backup FAILED: {{ instance }}" \
                      "The nightly application backup for {{ instance }} exited $1. It pushes to PBS datastore {{ _mp_backup_datastore }} as host/{{ _mp_backup_id }}. Read the failed job's output with: kubectl -n {{ k8s_namespace }} logs -l app.kubernetes.io/component=backup --all-containers --prefix"
                    return 0
                  }
                  trap 'on_exit $?' EXIT

                  # The client is installed at run time from Proxmox's own repository
                  # because no official image ships it and this lab has no registry to
                  # build one into. See the `backup.client` comment in
                  # vars/app-defaults/mixpost.yml for the options and the trade-off; the
                  # cost is that this job needs outbound network every time it runs.
                  apt-get update -qq
                  apt-get install -y -qq --no-install-recommends ca-certificates curl >/dev/null
                  install -d -m 0755 /etc/apt/keyrings
                  curl -fsSL "${PBS_KEY_URL}" -o /etc/apt/keyrings/proxmox-release.gpg
                  echo "deb [signed-by=/etc/apt/keyrings/proxmox-release.gpg] ${PBS_APT_REPO}" \
                    > /etc/apt/sources.list.d/pbs-client.list
                  apt-get update -qq
                  apt-get install -y -qq --no-install-recommends proxmox-backup-client >/dev/null

                  # Two archives in one snapshot, deliberately. They are one application's
                  # state at one moment and a restore needs both halves; filing them as one
                  # snapshot makes "which dump goes with which uploads" unanswerable by
                  # mistake.
                  #
                  # The dump is pushed UNCOMPRESSED. PBS chunks and compresses server-side
                  # and deduplicates across snapshots, so last night's SQL and tonight's
                  # SQL share almost every chunk. Gzipping first would produce a completely
                  # different byte stream each night and cost the deduplication that makes
                  # a nightly full dump affordable at all.
                  proxmox-backup-client backup \
                    database.pxar:/artifact \
                    storage.pxar:/app-storage \
                    --backup-id "${BACKUP_ID}"

                  # Retention. Pruning marks snapshots for removal; PBS reclaims the space
                  # in its own garbage collection, which PBS schedules and this platform
                  # does not drive.
                  proxmox-backup-client prune "host/${BACKUP_ID}" \
                    --keep-daily "${RETENTION_DAYS}"

                  echo "backup complete: host/${BACKUP_ID} in ${PBS_REPOSITORY##*:}"
              env:
                # Not secret, and useful in the log when a restore has to find this
                # snapshot again: an API token id is an identity, not a credential.
                - name: PBS_REPOSITORY
                  value: "{{ _mp_backup_repository }}"
                # PBS serves a self-signed certificate. Pinning its fingerprint is what
                # lets the client verify the server without a prompt it cannot answer from
                # a Job. It is read from PBS at DEPLOY time, so regenerating the PBS
                # certificate breaks this job until the deploy is re-run — which is stated
                # here because the failure would otherwise look like a network problem.
                - name: PBS_FINGERPRINT
                  value: "{{ _mp_backup_fingerprint }}"
                - name: PBS_PASSWORD
                  valueFrom:
                    secretKeyRef: { name: {{ instance }}-backup, key: pbs-password }
                - name: BACKUP_ID
                  value: "{{ _mp_backup_id }}"
                - name: RETENTION_DAYS
                  value: "{{ b.retention_days | int }}"
                - name: PBS_KEY_URL
                  value: "{{ b.client.key_url }}"
                - name: PBS_APT_REPO
                  value: "{{ b.client.repository }} {{ b.client.suite }} {{ b.client.component }}"
{% if _mp_ntfy_url | length > 0 %}
                - name: NTFY_URL
                  value: "{{ _mp_ntfy_url }}"
{% endif %}
{% if _mp_ntfy_token | length > 0 %}
                - name: NTFY_TOKEN
                  valueFrom:
                    secretKeyRef: { name: {{ instance }}-backup, key: ntfy-token }
{% endif %}
              volumeMounts:
                - name: artifact
                  mountPath: /artifact
                  readOnly: true
                # READ-ONLY, and not negotiable. This pod exists to copy the application's
                # uploads out; nothing it does should be able to change them. The volume is
                # ReadWriteOnce and the application pod holds it, which is fine — RWO is a
                # per-NODE constraint, and the node-pinned StorageClass guarantees this pod
                # lands on the same node as the pod already using it.
                - name: app-storage
                  mountPath: /app-storage
                  readOnly: true
              resources:
                requests:
                  cpu: 100m
                  memory: 256Mi

          volumes:
            - name: credentials
              secret:
                secretName: {{ instance }}-backup
                # 0444 rather than 0400. The mysql image may run as a non-root user, and a
                # root-owned 0400 file would then be unreadable — a permissions failure
                # that reads like a wrong password. The mysql client refuses only
                # WORLD-WRITABLE defaults files, so world-readable inside this pod's own
                # tmpfs is both accepted and safe: the file exists nowhere else.
                defaultMode: 0444
                items:
                  - key: my.cnf
                    path: my.cnf
            - name: artifact
              emptyDir:
                sizeLimit: {{ b.scratch_size | int }}Gi
            - name: app-storage
              persistentVolumeClaim:
                claimName: {{ instance }}-storage
                readOnly: true
